{"questions":[{"question_id":"mp9ygvhg","content":"<p>The correct statement(s) regarding sugars is(are) :</p>","chapter":"biomolecules","chapterGroup":"organic-chemistry","country":"in","difficulty":"medium","exam":"jee-advanced","examGroup":"jee","isBonus":false,"isOutOfSyllabus":false,"languages":["en"],"marks":4,"negMarks":1,"paperId":"jee-advanced-2026-paper-2-online","paperTitle":"JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online","permalink":"pthe-correct-statements-regarding-sugars-isarep-jee-advanced-chemistry-z3tgsyw0lwvecycu","question":{"en":{"comprehension":null,"direction":null,"content":"<p>The correct statement(s) regarding sugars is(are) :</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"<p>On treatment with HNO<sub>3</sub>, gluconic acid is oxidized to saccharic acid, whereas glucose is not oxidized to saccharic acid.</p>"},{"identifier":"B","content":"<p>Fructose gives a positive Fehling’s test because it isomerises to glucose and another aldohexose in the presence of Fehling’s reagent.</p>"},{"identifier":"C","content":"<p>Invert sugar is an equimolar mixture of D-glucose and D-fructose formed after hydrolysis of the corresponding disaccharide.</p>"},{"identifier":"D","content":"<p>Specific rotation of invert sugar is −40°.</p>"}],"correct_options":["B","C"],"answer":null,"explanation":"<p><b>Option (A)</b></p>\n<p><img alt=\"JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online Chemistry - Biomolecules Question 1 English Explanation 1\" src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;max-height:40vh;vertical-align:baseline\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/6y3zli1mpbdrq84/5c36dc4a-985e-44cc-8b54-f2f2c87e75d5/48ffc440-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbdrq85.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>Glucose, when oxidized at the aldehyde group (–CHO), forms <b>gluconic acid</b>. When it is oxidized more strongly at both ends (–CHO and –CH<sub>2</sub>OH), it forms <b>saccharic acid</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Option (B)</b></p>\n<p><img alt=\"JEE Advanced 2026 Paper 2 Online Chemistry - Biomolecules Question 1 English Explanation 2\" src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0 auto;max-height:40vh;vertical-align:baseline\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/6y3zli1mpbduclc/248312e6-469f-481a-9a16-9d55785a4793/91e54400-52d1-11f1-9482-3120031649b2/file-6y3zli1mpbducld.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"></p>\n<p>In a basic medium, fructose changes (tautomerises) into its isomers, <b>D-glucose</b> and <b>D-mannose</b>. </p>\n<p>Since glucose and mannose are reducing sugars, this explains why fructose also gives a positive test with Fehling’s solution. This solution contains a weak base (dilute NaOH), which allows the reaction to occur.</p>\n<p><b>Option (C)</b></p>\n<p>When sucrose is hydrolysed using an acid, it breaks down into <b>D-glucose</b> and <b>D-fructose</b> in equal amounts (1:1). This mixture is called <b>invert sugar</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Option (D)</b></p>\n<p>The <b>specific rotation</b> of these sugars is as follows:</p>\n<p>For D-fructose: <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"6.77ex\" height=\"1.785ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -707 2992.6 789\" xmlns